window.docContents[41].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Process.Types/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Process___Types":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Process › Types.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 5 headline theorems depend on it 216 of 326 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (34)structure Snapshot.Regiondef Snapshot.cellAtdef Snapshot.loadCellAtdef nulldef phdrdef phentdef phnumdef pageszdef basedef flagsdef entrydef uiddef euiddef giddef egiddef platformdef hwcapdef clktckdef securedef randomdef hwcap2def execfndef bytesOfStringstructure AuxvConfigdef defaultStackTopdef defaultStackSizedef defaultOraclestructure ProcessInitstructure ProcessInputsstructure ElfImagestructure ProcessOutcomedef traceStreamBytesdef ofStatedef ofSignal\n\nELF Process Types (Arm64.Trusted.Process.Types)* Layer / Role: Arm64 Trusted Computing Base (Role::Spec, definitions only, 0 theorems).* Purpose & Key Exports: Contiguous mapped byte regions (Snapshot.Region, Snapshot.cellAt), process-level environment inputs (AuxvTag, AuxvConfig, ProcessInit, ProcessInputs), parsed static ELF64 image (ElfImage), and observable process termination outcome (ProcessOutcome).* When to read: Read when writing or auditing whole-binary process specifications (ElfRunsTo, ElfCorrect, ElfSafe) or inspecting how argv, envp, stdin, initial files, and kernel/environment initialization choices (init : ProcessInit) are supplied to an ELF process.\n\n\n\nstructure Snapshot.RegionL32–40 ↗#One contiguous mapped byte region: bytes at [start, start + bytes.length), all with perm. The prefix [start, start + nonAnonLen) (0 by default) is backed by an ELF file mapping (anon := false in Snapshot.loadCellAt, matching Linux fs/binfmt_elf.c elf_map vs anonymous vm_brk_flags tail).\n\ndef Snapshot.cellAtL42–47 ↗#The permission and byte at a in the first region containing a, if any.\n\ndef Snapshot.loadCellAtL49–58 ↗#Process-loader cell lookup: like Snapshot.cellAt, clearing lazyFree := false and marking bytes in the file-backed prefix i < r.nonAnonLen with anon := false.\n\n\n\ndef nullL68–69 ↗#End of auxiliary vector (AT_NULL = 0).\n\ndef phdrL70–71 ↗#Address of program headers in memory (AT_PHDR = 3).\n\ndef phentL72–73 ↗#Size in bytes of one program header entry (AT_PHENT = 4).\n\ndef phnumL74–75 ↗#Number of program header entries (AT_PHNUM = 5).\n\ndef pageszL76–77 ↗#System page size in bytes (AT_PAGESZ = 6, 4096 on AArch64).\n\ndef baseL78–79 ↗#Base address of interpreter (AT_BASE = 7, 0 for static binaries).\n\ndef flagsL80–81 ↗#Flags (AT_FLAGS = 8, 0).\n\ndef entryL82–83 ↗#Entry point of executable (AT_ENTRY = 9).\n\ndef uidL84–85 ↗#Real user ID (AT_UID = 11).\n\ndef euidL86–87 ↗#Effective user ID (AT_EUID = 12).\n\ndef gidL88–89 ↗#Real group ID (AT_GID = 13).\n\ndef egidL90–91 ↗#Effective group ID (AT_EGID = 14).\n\ndef platformL92–93 ↗#Address of platform string \"aarch64\\0\" (AT_PLATFORM = 15).\n\ndef hwcapL94–95 ↗#CPU feature bitmask (AT_HWCAP = 16).\n\ndef clktckL96–97 ↗#Frequency of times() (AT_CLKTCK = 17, 100 Hz).\n\ndef secureL98–99 ↗#Boolean secure-execution mode flag (AT_SECURE = 23, 0 for normal execution).\n\ndef randomL100–101 ↗#Address of 16 bytes of random seed data (AT_RANDOM = 25).\n\ndef hwcap2L102–103 ↗#Extended CPU feature bitmask (AT_HWCAP2 = 26).\n\ndef execfnL104–105 ↗#Address of filename string executed (AT_EXECFN = 31).\n\n\n\nProcess Environment Inputs (AuxvConfig, ProcessInit, ProcessInputs)\n\ndef bytesOfStringL111–113 ↗#Convert an ASCII String into List (BitVec 8) (kernel-reducible via String.toList).\n\nstructure AuxvConfigL115–138 ↗#Deterministic auxiliary-vector parameters (no ASLR). Image-derived entries (AT_PHDR, AT_PHENT, AT_PHNUM, AT_PAGESZ, AT_BASE, AT_ENTRY, AT_PLATFORM, AT_RANDOM, AT_EXECFN, AT_NULL) are populated automatically by the ELF loader from ElfImage and the stack layout.\n\ndef defaultStackTopL140–142 ↗#Default top of the user stack (0x0000_7fff_ffff_0000, page-aligned, below the canonical user limit and separate from fixed ET_EXEC load addresses).\n\ndef defaultStackSizeL144–146 ↗#Default user stack mapping size (8 MiB = 2048 4 KiB pages; Linux _STK_LIM in include/uapi/linux/resource.h).\n\ndef defaultOracleL148–152 ↗#Default deterministic anonymous mmap base (0x0000_6000_0000_0000), placed well above fixed ET_EXEC segments and brk heap and well below the user stack. Returning successive 64 KiB slots allows multiple anonymous mmap calls when inputs.init.oracle is not overridden.\n\nstructure ProcessInitL154–187 ↗#Nondeterministic kernel, hardware, and environment choices made at Linux execve(2) process-initialization time (fs/exec.c bprm_mm_init, fs/binfmt_elf.c setup_arg_pages + create_elf_tables, arch/arm64/include/asm/elf.h randomize_stack_top):* auxv: auxiliary-vector seed, credential, and CPU-feature configuration (AT_RANDOM 16-byte buffer, AT_HWCAP/AT_HWCAP2, AT_UID/AT_EUID/AT_GID/AT_EGID, AT_SECURE, and optional arch tags),* stackTop: page-aligned top of the initial user stack ([stackTop - stackSize, stackTop)),* stackSize: byte size of the initial user stack mapping (RLIMIT_STACK),* physMemAvail: available physical RAM budget in bytes (1 GiB = 2 ^ 30 by default) for dynamic writable anonymous mappings (Perm.rw) on top of initial PT_LOAD segments and the mapped user stack [stackTop - stackSize, stackTop),* oracle: nondeterministic kernel syscall and hardware store-exclusive (exclFails) oracle copied into State.oracle and State.exclFails by ElfImage.loadState.\n\nstructure ProcessInputsL189–214 ↗#Complete system inputs to a Linux AArch64 ELF process, separating caller-supplied / filesystem inputs from kernel/environment initialization choices (init : ProcessInit):* User / caller-supplied inputs: command-line arguments (argv), environment variables (envp), executed filename (execfn for AT_EXECFN), standard input byte stream (stdin), and initial working-directory filesystem (files, dirs).* Nondeterministic kernel/environment init record (init : ProcessInit): auxiliary-vector parameters (init.auxv), initial stack geometry (init.stackTop, init.stackSize), available physical memory (init.physMemAvail), and syscall/hardware oracle (init.oracle, including init.oracle.exclFails).\n\n6 declarations not shown (lines 218–240): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Process.ProcessInputs.auxv, Arm64.Process.ProcessInputs.stackTop, Arm64.Process.ProcessInputs.stackSize, Arm64.Process.ProcessInputs.physMemAvail, Arm64.Process.ProcessInputs.oracle, Arm64.Process.ProcessInputs.ofStrings\n\nParsed Static ELF64 Image (ElfImage)\n\nstructure ElfImageL246–262 ↗#A validated, statically linked AArch64 ELF64 executable image (ET_EXEC):* entry: initial program counter (e_entry),* phdrVaddr: virtual address of the ELF program header table in memory (AT_PHDR),* phent: byte size of each program header entry (e_phentsize = 56),* phnum: number of program header entries (e_phnum),* segments: page-aligned, non-overlapping PT_LOAD segments in ascending address order,* brkStart: page-aligned end of the highest PT_LOAD segment (mm->start_brk),* symbols: optional (name, vaddr) symbol table extracted from the binary.\n\nObservable Process Outcome (ProcessOutcome)\n\nstructure ProcessOutcomeL266–281 ↗#Observable outcome of a terminated process (SYS_exit, SYS_exit_group, or fatal signal ExecStatus.Signaled sig):* exitCode: exit status (code for .Exited code; BitVec.ofNat 64 (128 + sig.toNat) for .Signaled sig, matching POSIX shell $? and qemu-aarch64 exit code 134 on SIGABRT),* signal: none on normal exit/exit_group, or some sig.toNat (some 6 for SIGABRT) on fatal signal death so specifications can distinguish abort() from exit(134),* stdout: ordered sequence of bytes written to fd = 1 (OutputStream.stdout),* stderr: ordered sequence of bytes written to fd = 2 (OutputStream.stderr),* fs: final file-system state (State.fs).\n\n\n\ndef traceStreamBytesL285–291 ↗#Extract all bytes written to stream from an execution trace.\n\ndef ofStateL293–299 ↗#Project the observable ProcessOutcome from a terminated .Exited code state sf.\n\ndef ofSignalL301–308 ↗#Project the observable ProcessOutcome from a signal-terminated .Signaled sig state sf, reporting shell / qemu-aarch64 status 128 + sig.toNat and signal := some sig.toNat.\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 310–322): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Process.ProcessOutcome.ofTerminated?, Arm64.Process.ProcessOutcome.fileBytes?\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Process/Types.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Process.Types/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Process___Types"}});